# 2. FILE / SEGMENT MODEL (segments.ts) — invariants are law

Segment {id, start, end (exclusive), cursor, ownerId|null, state: QUEUED|ACTIVE|DONE}. Invariants, asserted every tick in dev mode and in Diagnostics:
I1 segments tile [0,fileSize) exactly: sorted by start, seg[i].end === seg[i+1].start, first.start=0, last.end=fileSize.
I2 start <= cursor <= end.
I3 every boundary except fileSize is a multiple of BLOCK.
I4 a block is counted received exactly once (coverage bitmap Uint8Array, one byte per BLOCK; fileSize up to 20 GB => at most 305,176 entries). Duplicate writes are counted as `duplicateBytes` and discarded; gaps and overlaps must be 0 at completion.
I5 sum of received bytes === sum of (cursor - start) over segments.
The completion check requires I1..I5 and 0 gaps. A violation sets the transfer to ERROR state with a diagnostic panel (not a crash).

Initial split: N = number of ACTIVE donors; segments are sized proportional to current EWMA weights (equal weights if none yet), aligned to BLOCK, min segment = 1 MiB. If fileSize < 4 MiB, do NOT split; one segment to the best donor.

Work-stealing (aria2 split): when a donor becomes idle and the queue has no QUEUED segment, choose the victim = ACTIVE segment with the largest remaining bytes. remaining = end - cursor. If remaining < 2*MIN_SPLIT (MIN_SPLIT=512 KiB), do nothing (never steal tiny tails). Otherwise split point:
- strategy "halve": splitAt = alignUp(cursor + remaining/2, BLOCK)
- strategy "proportional" (default): f = thiefEwma/(thiefEwma+victimEwma) clamped [0.25, 0.75]; splitAt = alignDown(end - f*remaining, BLOCK).
Then clamp splitAt into [cursor + MIN_SPLIT, end - MIN_SPLIT] (if impossible, abort the split). Victim.end = splitAt; the new segment [splitAt, oldEnd) goes to the thief. Straggler duplication in the last 2 % of the file: if a segment's remaining < 2 MiB and its owner is slower than 0.5x the fastest idle donor, allow an endgame duplicate request (counts duplicateBytes when both land; first writer wins).

Failure handling: when a donor disconnects, stalls > 5 s with no bytes, or errors, its segment keeps the bytes already received (cursor), becomes QUEUED with the highest priority at the head of the queue, ownerId=null. Retries use exponential backoff with full jitter: delay = random(0, min(8 s, 0.5 s * 2^attempt)), max 5 attempts per segment attempt-chain; after 5 consecutive failures on a donor, move that donor to QUARANTINED (section 4). A segment is never failed permanently because a donor failed; only if ALL donors are unavailable for 120 s does the transfer become "Needs attention" (still resumable).

Request overhead model: each (re)start of a segment costs 1 RTT before the first byte; a fresh connection adds 1.5 RTT (TCP) + 1 RTT (TLS 1.3). Connections are reused (keep-alive) across consecutive segments on the same donor unless a reset occurred. Track per-donor `connReuseRatio` and global open sockets vs a cap (default 4 per donor, 64 total; Settings). Exceeding a cap queues the request and increments `portPressure` shown as a warning chip when > 80 % of the cap (this models ephemeral-port exhaustion).

# 3. SCHEDULERS (src/engine/schedulers) — implement all four, switchable live

Common interface: pickAssignment(ctx): {donorId, segmentId, size} | null, called whenever a donor is idle or a segment finishes. Inputs: donor stats (ewmaTputBps, ewmaRttMs, demotionMultiplier, state, thompson posterior), segment queue, mode.

EWMA everywhere: x_hat = alpha*x + (1-alpha)*x_hat with alpha = 0.2 (Setting, range 0.05-0.5), first sample initializes x_hat. Throughput sample = bytes received in the last 500 ms / 0.5 s. RTT sample every 1 s from the telemetry.

1. NAIVE_RR (baseline, the "negative bonding" demo): fixed-size chunks (512 KiB) assigned round-robin to donors in order, ignoring speed and RTT. Works but shows straggler and head-of-line damage in Stream mode.
2. DWRR: weight_i = ewmaTput_i * rttPenalty_i * demotion_i, with rttPenalty_i = 1/(1 + 0.5*max(0,(rtt_i - rttMin)/rttMin)). Normalize weights. Quantum_i = weight_i * 256 KiB * N. Deficit counter D_i += Quantum_i per round; a donor receives a chunk of size c (default 512 KiB) if D_i >= c, then D_i -= c. Reset D_i to 0 when a donor goes non-ACTIVE. Cold start (no samples): equal weights.
3. ECF (Earliest Completion First), used for Stream mode: for the next in-order chunk, estimate completion_i = max(availableAt_i, now) + rtt_i + chunkBytes*8/ewmaTput_i. Assign to argmin. ECF waiting rule: if the fastest donor f is busy but availableAt_f + completion time for the chunk < completion on the best idle donor s, then WAIT (do not assign to s). This prevents a slow path from stalling the in-order reorder buffer.
4. ADAPTIVE (Thompson Sampling): each donor keeps Beta(a,b), init (1,1). After each completed chunk the reward r=1 if its goodput >= 0.8 * median goodput of ACTIVE donors over the last 10 s and no error occurred, else 0. Update with discount gamma = 0.98: a = gamma*a + r; b = gamma*b + (1-r); floor a,b at 1. On assignment sample theta_i ~ Beta(a_i,b_i) with the seeded PRNG; choose the donor with the highest theta_i * demotion_i among idle donors; chunk size = clamp(512 KiB * (0.5 + theta_i), 256 KiB, 4 MiB), aligned to BLOCK. Compute cost per decision must stay < 0.05 ms (measure with performance.now() ONLY inside Diagnostics for display, never in engine logic). Default scheduler = ADAPTIVE with work-stealing on.

Modes:
- TURBO (default): chunks are range segments written at arbitrary offsets (WriteAt semantics); reassembly order is irrelevant; work-stealing active.
- STREAM: bytes must be consumed in order (video playback). A reorder buffer holds out-of-order bytes; maintain `reorderBufferBytes` and `holStallMs` (time the consumer waited for the next in-order byte while later bytes were already buffered). The consumer drains at a playback bitrate setting (default 6 Mbps). Work-stealing is disabled; ECF is the recommended scheduler and shown as "Recommended" chip. If the reorder buffer exceeds 64 MiB, pause assigning new chunks to donors whose chunks are beyond the window (back-pressure).

Server behavior presets for a transfer (affects the engine; show these in the New transfer dialog):
- "Range CDN" (Accept-Ranges: bytes, 206 Partial Content): normal.
- "No range support" (200 only): the engine detects it on the probe, DISABLES splitting, assigns the whole file to the single best-score donor, and shows an info banner. Failure of that donor resumes from scratch (no resume possible), and the UI states this.
- "IP-locked CDN token": the origin ties the signed URL to the first egress IP (the donor that did the probe). Range requests via other donors return 403. The engine must detect 403 on non-probe donors after 2 attempts, stop using them for this transfer, pin to the probe donor, and show the banner "This server locks downloads to one IP. Using one donor." with a "Why?" dialog explaining it.
- "Flaky origin": 3 % of range requests return 503; handled by the retry rule (backoff with jitter). The retries counter is visible.

# 4. DONOR STATE MACHINE (quota.ts)

States: DISCOVERED, HANDSHAKING, ACTIVE, DRAINING, QUARANTINED, DISCONNECTED. Implement a transition table; any transition not in the table THROWS a typed error in dev and is logged + ignored in prod (never corrupts state). Table:
DISCOVERED -> HANDSHAKING (user taps Connect, or auto-connect setting) ; HANDSHAKING -> ACTIVE (after 0.6-1.4 s simulated mTLS/Noise handshake; fail 3 % if "Flaky handshakes" scenario) ; HANDSHAKING -> DISCONNECTED (timeout 5 s) ; ACTIVE -> DRAINING (usedBytes >= 90 % of quota, OR battery <= 15 % and not charging, OR user taps Pause/Drain) ; ACTIVE -> QUARANTINED (5 consecutive failures) ; ACTIVE -> DISCONNECTED (heartbeat lost, user disconnect, quota exhausted, battery 0) ; DRAINING -> DISCONNECTED (in-flight chunk finished, or usedBytes >= 100 % of quota — the in-flight chunk is truncated exactly at the quota boundary and the remainder is requeued) ; DRAINING -> ACTIVE (user Resume, only if quota < 90 % and battery > 20 %) ; QUARANTINED -> ACTIVE (after backoff 5 s, 10 s, 20 s ... capped 60 s, rejoin on probation with demotion multiplier 0.5 for 30 s) ; DISCONNECTED -> DISCOVERED (heartbeat returns).
Quota is enforced on BYTES THROUGH THE DONOR (counted per tick before the byte is credited). usedBytes must never exceed quotaBytes (invariant I6, asserted). The host donor's quota also feeds the laptop's own line if the user ticks "Use my phone too".
Battery model: drain 0.0015 %/s idle, +0.0008 %/s per Mbps relayed, charging +0.01 %/s. Battery <= 15 % and unplugged triggers DRAINING with a snackbar. OEM killer: if the donor has `oemKiller` on (Samsung/Xiaomi preset) and the foreground-service toggle is off, the app is silently killed at t_start + 180 s (heartbeat stops; engine reacts via the 6 s heartbeat timeout). With the foreground-service toggle on, it survives. This is shown on the donor detail as a "Background survival" row with exact guidance (FOREGROUND_SERVICE_TYPE_DATA_SYNC, PARTIAL_WAKE_LOCK, WIFI_MODE_FULL_HIGH_PERF, dontkillmyapp.com).
Privacy/Safety rule shown in UI: donors can set a domain blocklist (SNI policy). Simulated origin hostnames matching a donor's blocklist cause that donor to be skipped for that transfer (e.g., "*.torrent-site.example"). Keep it as a text list per donor with validation (hostname patterns, one per line, max 50, trim, dedupe, lowercase).

# 5. PEARSON SAME-TOWER DETECTOR (pearson.ts)

pearson(x,y): returns NaN if lengths differ, n < 20, or either variance < 1e-9. Never returns a value outside [-1,1] (clamp float error). Compute with numerically stable two-pass math.
Detection loop every 5.0 s of sim time, window = last 30 one-second cellular-RTT samples per donor (use donor-reported cellRtt, NOT total path RTT, so a shared Wi-Fi bottleneck cannot cause false positives). Compare only donors of the SAME carrier (different carriers cannot share radio resources; their cells show "n/a"). A pair is FLAGGED after r >= 0.75 for 3 consecutive evaluations and UNFLAGGED after r < 0.55 for 3 consecutive evaluations (hysteresis). Build connected components of flagged pairs; in each component keep the donor with the highest ewmaTput as PRIMARY and set demotion_i = 0.25 for the others (never disconnect by default; Settings has an "Auto-disconnect colliding donors" switch, OFF by default). When flags clear, demotion returns to 1 gradually (+0.25 per evaluation). The Insights screen shows the 6x6 matrix heatmap (diverging color, diagonal = 1, NaN = hatched "not enough data"), a legend, and a plain-language explanation. Add the honest caveat in the UI: "Correlation suggests, not proves, a shared tower."
The detector must recognize the demo roster's D1/D2 (both Jio, T1) within 45 s at 1x speed with seed 20260101 (acceptance test).

